{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310855,"nonce":"OK2p/Io9IW0iCDPgIRsY3+PwBVc=","title":"2026-09-05 — the five open defects were closed in one sweep (5-agent workflow + adversarial verify). Battery 439 -\u003e 449, LOST 0, GAINED 10. ...","message":"**Battery at 5902a5c1: 449/1240** vs the 439 baseline. GAINED 10 (Issue2835, 3063, 3447, 3512, 3635,\n4000, 6587, 6833, 6942, 7942), LOST 0, MOVED 3 (Issue4068, Issue4675, TestOps: CRASH -\u003e WRONG_ANSWER,\neach stable on re-run — programs that used to die now run and expose pre-existing wrong answers).\nHarness 19 PASS / 3 known bugs unchanged; nue llama-chat compiles and runs.\n\n1. **Loop counters (e47ef3dd).** `emit_iterator_while_loop` asks which variables the body modifies via\n   `find_modified_variables_in_statement`, which records assignments only — `n++` is invisible, so the\n   counter got no slot, was re-read from its entry value every iteration, and the loop EXIT read a\n   register defined inside the body (\"Argument IrId(N) not found in value_map\"). The sibling handle\n   loop already had `collect_incremented_symbols`; both now use it (`pub(super)`).\n   COUPLED AND ESSENTIAL: `break`/`continue` never stored back to the slots in EITHER slot loop, so\n   the slot fix alone would have turned a compile error into a SILENT wrong answer (`n++; if (x==3)\n   break` -\u003e 1 instead of 3). `LoopContext` now carries `carried_slots` and both edges flush them via\n   `flush_carried_slots`. Probe `$SP/brk` covers break/continue on every loop shape.\n   REJECTED: widening `find_modified_variables_in_expression` itself — it also feeds while/do-while's\n   `assigned_in_body`, switch merges and the if-merge (5 call sites); the agent measured the local\n   collector as sufficient and it is what the sibling path already does.\n2. **`Null\u003cBool\u003e` (1dbc9397).** A container erases values into a u64 slot, so `get` hands a Bool back\n   as 0/1 in an i64 and `box_raw_optional_result` picked the box from the REGISTER (Int) — printed `1`\n   and every unbox read false. The DECLARED inner type now picks the box kind.\n3. **Map rendering (c845cdbb).** `haxe_{string,int,object}map_to_string` hold one u64 per value and\n   printed it as an integer (`Map\u003cInt,String\u003e` -\u003e `[1 =\u003e 36506716128]`; ObjectMap keys as hex). Added\n   `*_to_string_typed` taking the value's (and an object map's key's) Std.string tag — the SAME tag\n   `Array.join` uses (`haxe_array_join_typed`) — registered in plugin_impl.rs, chosen by\n   `try_lower_map_to_string` from the receiver's type args. Separately `Std.string(map)` printed\n   `\u003cunknown type N\u003e` because `convert_to_string_with_hint` never consulted the class's own\n   `toString`; it does now (also fixes `Std.string` of a user class with toString).\n4. **Issue12700 (dee41319) — the big one, +9 rows.** A method-level generic erases its params to I64\n   and reads them through a tag the INLINER resolves per call site (`type_param_tag_fixups`). The\n   inliner bound each erased param straight to the caller's register, so an f64 argument arrived as a\n   float where the by-tag helpers read an int. Fix, all in `inline_call_site`: record EVERY erased\n   slot (the old scan `break`s at the first, missing `eq\u003cT\u003e(v:T, v2:T)`'s second), and bind an erased\n   slot to a BitCast of the argument to I64 (f32: Cast then BitCast), splicing the coercions where the\n   call stood so they dominate every use. NOT the reverted attempt's approach — see\n   [[project-generic-method-type-args-never-inferred]] for why that one failed.\n5. **ov1 (5902a5c1).** `Map\u003cK,V\u003e` with a type-parameter key can never pick a container, so the slot\n   kept the `@:multiType` abstract; the abstract's methods dispatch through its underlying\n   `IMap\u003cK,V\u003e` (a fat pointer) while producers hand over a bare container pointer -\u003e SIGSEGV. Such a\n   slot is now typed as that interface (`multitype_map_underlying_imap`, all three annotation sites),\n   and a map LITERAL (whose type is `TypeKind::Map`, naming no class) resolves its container class at\n   the wrap site via `map_container_class_symbol`.\n\n- `for (x in arr.iterator())` typed its element from the CALL's type (the iterator class, which cannot\n  say what it holds) -\u003e Dynamic -\u003e a Bool printed as `1` and `if (x)` was false. FIX:\n  `element_type_from_iterator_call` takes the element from the `iterator()` RECEIVER (Array\u003cT\u003e -\u003e T,\n  Map\u003cK,V\u003e -\u003e V, a one-parameter Class/Interface -\u003e its argument), which is what `for (x in xs)`\n  already binds. Two earlier attempts were inert and reverted (kept at `$SP/loops_with_classarg.rs`,\n  `$SP/calls_with_aliasnode.rs`): resolving the element from the ITERATOR class's type args, and\n  reading a typedef's params off its alias node.\n- The same loop needed the declared-locals fix the handle loop already had, or a conditional `n++` in\n  its body stayed lost even with the slot (`$SP/boolC`, `$SP/boolD` now MATCH).\n\n`var it:Iterator\u003cBool\u003e = arr.iterator(); while (it.hasNext())` iterated ZERO times (also `for (x in\nit)`). Chain: `Array.iterator()` is typed **Dynamic** by the typer (infer.rs), and `Iterator\u003cT\u003e`\nlowers to a CLASS-shaped node, so the Let/Assign coercion chain ran `maybe_unbox_value`\n(boxing.rs:613) BEFORE the handle wrap (statements.rs:323 / :829). The unbox emitted\n`haxe_unbox_reference_ptr` on a raw ArrayIterator — reading its first word as a box payload — and\nthe fresh register carried NO class hint, so `maybe_wrap_for_iter_protocol` -\u003e `iter_source_from_hint`\ncould no longer classify it and built no handle; the consumer's tag check then failed and the loop\nwas skipped. Producer-side `wrap_stdlib_iter_result` had already declined for the same reason\n(`iter_protocol_of(Dynamic)` = None). FIX (one guard): `maybe_unbox_value` returns the value untouched\nwhen `iter_protocol_of(target_ty)` is Some — such a target is structural, so the value is a raw\ncollection or iterator, never a box. Probes `$SP/typedit`, `$SP/elem` now MATCH.\nEVIDENCE THAT ISOLATED IT: the same value passed to a PARAMETER typed `Iterator\u003cBool\u003e` always worked\n(that site wraps before any unbox), and `for (x in arr.iterator())` worked (it resolves hasNext/next\nby class hint, no handle involved).\n\nFixing the fresh-compile path exposed a DIFFERENT defect in the SAME shape, pre-existing (the\n721e10d0 reference SIGSEGVs identically): with the stdlib cache WARM, `Iterator\u003cBool\u003e` comes back as\na `TypeKind::TypeAlias` whose declaration carries NO parameter list, so `alias_bindings` binds\nnothing, `next()` resolves to the bare parameter, the element falls back to Dynamic, and\n`for (x in it)` renders each element through the reference path — SIGSEGV in `haxe_std_string_ptr`\nreading 0x1 (a Bool). FIX: when the resolved element is still a bare type parameter, take the\nreceiver's SOLE type argument (a one-parameter protocol has exactly one thing it can stand for; a\ntwo-parameter iterator answers with a pair, so it never reaches there).\nATTEMPT THAT FAILED TWICE (do not retry): reading the typedef's parameter list off its alias node in\n`alias_bindings` — inert in BOTH spellings, reverted both times ($SP/calls_with_aliasnode.rs).\n\n**METHOD GAP — FIXED 2026-09-05 (tooling, not the compiler).** `gate_final.sh` now defines the probe\nlist ONCE and runs it through both compile paths, plus the harness case dirs in the cached path;\n`run_probe.sh` takes `MODE` (\"--no-cache\" = fresh, \"\" = cached) and tags each line. Each path keeps a\ndiff baseline (`probe_baseline_{fresh,cached}.txt`) and the gate prints `** NEW DIFFS: ... **` when a\nshape starts differing, so nothing depends on remembering to run a second command. Map-order probes (boolA, boolB, brk) flip between runs, so they sit in `probe_noisy.txt` and are held out of the NEW-DIFFS alarm — otherwise the tripwire cries wolf every gate. First run recorded\n1 fresh diff (map order) and 15 cached ones, THREE of which are real cached-only defects it found\nimmediately: [[bugs-cached-spelling-only-defects]]. Was: `run_probe.sh`, the\nconformance runner and the harness all pass `--no-cache`, so the CACHED spelling of every structural\ntypedef went untested all session. `$SP/cached_sweep.sh \u003cprobe\u003e...` runs the same probes with the\ncache warm and compares against the oracle; it found this crash immediately and now reports\nCACHED-MATCH for 20 of 21 probes (brk's only diff is map ORDER). RUN IT alongside the gate whenever\na change touches structural types, `iter_protocol_of`, or anything the stdlib snapshot restores.\n\n- Map iteration ORDER differs from Haxe's (`$SP/brk` handle_brk, `$SP/boolA`, `$SP/boolB`, mapkeys3):\n  HashMap order, values all correct.\n- MOVED rows, now two (Issue4068, TestOps: CRASH -\u003e WRONG_ANSWER, stable; Issue4675 left the set):\n  programs that used to die now run and show pre-existing wrong answers.\n- Map iteration ORDER differs from Haxe's (`$SP/brk` handle_brk, `$SP/boolB`, mapkeys3) — HashMap\n  order, not a value defect.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_open_defect_sweep_2026_09_05` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}